05:01.000 --> 05:04.000
I want to quickly go through the course agenda.

05:04.000 --> 05:08.000
So I'm going to explain to you why Python.

05:08.000 --> 05:10.000
Why you might want to reach for Python.

05:10.000 --> 05:17.000
Why you might want to choose Python for your project and talk a little bit about some of the philosophies and Python's design principles.

05:17.000 --> 05:20.000
Then we're going to move on to data types.

05:20.000 --> 05:22.000
Python is a dynamic language.

05:22.000 --> 05:23.000
What does that mean?

05:23.000 --> 05:28.000
We're going to learn how to work with numbers, strings, lists, and more.

05:28.000 --> 05:33.000
Then we're going to move on to some of the more advanced data types that Python offers,

05:33.000 --> 05:36.000
and those are sets, tuples, and dictionaries.

05:36.000 --> 05:44.000
These are container objects that let us organize other types of objects in different ways into other kinds of data structures.

05:44.000 --> 05:49.000
Then we're going to move on to functions, loops, and logic.

05:49.000 --> 05:54.000
Functions help us organize our code in a way that's more readable and reusable,

05:54.000 --> 05:57.000
and they accept arguments and defaults as needed.

05:57.000 --> 06:02.000
And loops are going to take us, let us take action on collections of different items.

06:02.000 --> 06:06.000
Boolean logic is going to allow us to control the flow of our program.

06:06.000 --> 06:11.000
We're going to cover some practical applications of what we've learned so far,

06:11.000 --> 06:16.000
including using advanced looping types like list comprehension,

06:16.000 --> 06:22.000
converting between different types and Python, which I believe is really one of its absolute strengths.

06:22.000 --> 06:26.000
And we're also going to cover how to work with files.

06:26.000 --> 06:33.000
Next is going to be object-oriented Python, which allows us to represent our code in a little bit of a different way.

06:33.000 --> 06:39.000
We can define reusable classes and define relationships between different objects,

06:39.000 --> 06:42.000
and build modular and reusable code.

06:42.000 --> 06:45.000
We're also going to cover libraries and modules.

06:45.000 --> 06:49.000
So now that you have all the foundational understanding of how Python works,

06:49.000 --> 06:53.000
you're going to learn how to structure your code and work with different libraries,

06:53.000 --> 06:57.000
both the standard library and external third-party packages.

06:57.000 --> 07:00.000
And then in the last chapter, we're going to cover bug frameworks,

07:00.000 --> 07:03.000
and we're going to talk about Django in particular.

07:03.000 --> 07:08.000
So we're going to practice all of our new Python knowledge by building a basic blog,

07:08.000 --> 07:13.000
a basic web application with some unit tests and a database.

07:14.000 --> 07:20.000
This is going to give you a great tour of everything that's available in Python.

07:20.000 --> 07:24.000
You might not be an expert in any one of these areas by the end of the day,

07:24.000 --> 07:31.000
but you will have the tools available to learn and discover more.

